FL SO", "Sumter County GA SO", "Taylor MI PD", "Tinley Park IL PD", "Tipp City OH PD", "Tippecanoe County IN SO", "Toledo OH PD", "Treasure Island FL PD", "Union County KY SO", "University of Louisville KY PD", "Van Buren MI PD", "Vandalia OH PD", "Vanderburgh County IN SO", "Venice FL PD", "Verona WI PD [Inactive]", "Villa Park IL PD", "Village of Turtle Lake WI PD", "Walworth County WI SO", "Warner Robins GA PD", "Washburn County WI SO", "Washington County SO - GA", "Washington IA PD", "Washington Island WI PD", "Washington MO PD", "Waukesha County WI SO", "Waukon IA PD", "Wells County IN SO", "West Des Moines IA PD", "West Lafayette IN PD", "White County IN SO", "Whitefish Bay WI PD", "Whitestown IN PD", "Whitley County IN Prosector", "Wichita Falls TX PD", "Wilkinson County GA SO", "Winnebago County IL SO", "Winnebago County WI SO", "Wood County WI SO", "Wyoming MI PD"], "detected": "License Plates, Vehicles", "not_detected": "Facial recognition, People, Gender, Race", "acceptable_use": "Data is used for law enforcement purposes only. Data is owned by University of Wisconsin - Madison WI PD and is never sold to 3rd parties. Audit reports and event logs are accessible to system administrators to ensure the system is used appropriately.", "prohibited_uses": "Immigration enforcement, traffic enforcement, harassment or intimidation, usage based solely on a protected class (i.e. race, sex, religion), Personal use.", "access_policy": "All system access requires a valid reason and is stored indefinitely.", "more_info": {"Recent Success Story": "Flock Safety technology provides an additional tool to enhance investigative capabilities and support campus safety during critical incidents. It\u2019s one of many tools our department uses \u2014 it\u2019s not a replacement for officers, community partnerships, or traditional policing. Below is an instance where Flock Safety technology was instrumental in providing valuable information for an active investigation.\n\nArboretum Battery/Strangulation Incident:\nOn the morning of Monday, March 31, 2025, UWPD received a report of an aggravated assault that occurred in the UW Arboretum. The victim stated they were strangled and forced to remain in a vehicle during the incident. The suspect was unknown at the time of report.\nA description of a possible suspect vehicle was received later that morning. A search for these vehicle characteristics was promptly performed on the Flock Safety Platform and an image was identified which captured a vehicle with similar characteristics leaving the area shortly after the incident occurred.\nThis image provided a full license plate and, through use of additional investigative resources, the vehicle's involvement in the incident was able to be confirmed. This new lead allowed for possible suspects to be identified and further investigated, which ultimately resulted in the suspect being located and arrested.\nIn addition, through the course of the investigation, a second unknown vehicle was identified as aiding the suspect following the incident. Utilizing this vehicle description, investigators were able to identify only one vehicle in the area at that time with similar characteristics. A full plate was provided by the Flock Safety Platform, eventually leading to the identification of two other involved individuals."}}
